--TEST--
`Core\Queue::push` refuses a `secret` in its `args` payload, bare and inside the literal written at the call — `rule:security/secret-sinks-refuse`'s serialiser sink, one member further on
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:security/secret-sinks-refuse`'s serialiser bullet says an encoded
// document is on its way to a response, a log or a queue. This is the queue
// spelled out: `push` encodes `args` into a durable row with the same encoder
// `Core\Json::encode` uses, and a worker process decodes it later, so the
// refusal is the encoder's and reports the encoder's code.
//
// It is a call-site rule for `Core\Log::write`'s reason twice over: `args` is
// declared `mixed`, which a `secret string` satisfies, and the bag itself
// arrives as the registry's own shape, so the written option is the last place
// the qualifier is visible at all.
secret string $token = "literal";
string $to = "a@b";

// The accepted half first, so a position that stops being accepted fails here
// rather than as a missing refusal. Nothing below runs — a case with an error
// section never reaches its output — so these are compile-time claims only.
Core\Queue::push("send-mail.nvs", {args: ["to" => $to]});
Core\Queue::push("send-mail.nvs", {queue: "mail"});

// The bare payload.
Core\Queue::push("send-mail.nvs", {args: $token});

// And the shape the rule exists for: one credential among public fields, in
// both container spellings, each read off the written literal because an
// element checked against a `mixed` expectation carries nothing of its own.
Core\Queue::push("send-mail.nvs", {args: ["to" => $to, "token" => $token]});
Core\Queue::push("send-mail.nvs", {args: {to: $to, token: $token}});
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0791]: a `secret`-qualified value cannot be passed to `Core\Queue::push`; the `args` payload is encoded into a durable row that a worker process decodes later, and a stored job is not the credential being used
   --> case.nvs:22:42
